WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.687
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.987 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:18.212
Unit forty-six: Language Change. In this unit, you will describe how and why languages change using complex noun phrases.

4
00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:20.496
language change.

5
00:00:21.196 --> 00:00:25.274
language change means the gradual development of a language over time.

6
00:00:25.574 --> 00:00:29.710
Language change is natural; no living language stands still.

7
00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.875
Your turn. Say: language change.

8
00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:37.890
language change.

9
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:40.012
neologism.

10
00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:45.556
neologism means a newly invented word or expression.

11
00:00:45.856 --> 00:00:49.620
'Selfie' was a neologism in 2013.

12
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.762
Your turn. Say: neologism.

13
00:00:56.462 --> 00:00:57.533
neologism.

14
00:00:58.517 --> 00:00:59.889
linguistic drift.

15
00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:05.735
linguistic drift means slow, gradual change in a language's sounds and structure.

16
00:01:06.035 --> 00:01:11.053
Linguistic drift explains why Old English is unreadable today.

17
00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:15.253
Your turn. Say: linguistic drift.

18
00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:19.326
linguistic drift.

19
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:21.260
borrowing.

20
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.888
borrowing means taking words from another language.

21
00:01:25.188 --> 00:01:27.675
Khmer 'kafe' is a borrowing from French.

22
00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:31.387
Your turn. Say: borrowing.

23
00:01:34.087 --> 00:01:35.007
borrowing.

24
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:37.436
grammaticalisation.

25
00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:43.282
grammaticalisation means the process by which ordinary words become grammar words.

26
00:01:43.582 --> 00:01:46.881
'Going to' for the future is an example of grammaticalisation.

27
00:01:47.581 --> 00:01:50.803
Your turn. Say: grammaticalisation.

28
00:01:53.503 --> 00:01:54.875
grammaticalisation.

29
00:01:55.883 --> 00:01:57.268
semantic change.

30
00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:01.221
semantic change means a change in what a word means.

31
00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:05.239
'Nice' once meant 'foolish': a dramatic semantic change.

32
00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:09.822
Your turn. Say: semantic change.

33
00:02:12.522 --> 00:02:14.034
semantic change.

34
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.423
slang evolution.

35
00:02:17.123 --> 00:02:23.418
slang evolution means the fast change of informal words among groups such as teenagers.

36
00:02:23.718 --> 00:02:28.016
Slang evolution makes yesterday's 'cool' word sound old-fashioned.

37
00:02:28.716 --> 00:02:32.030
Your turn. Say: slang evolution.

38
00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.091
slang evolution.

39
00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:38.746
language contact.

40
00:02:39.446 --> 00:02:44.801
language contact means the meeting of languages through trade, migration or conquest.

41
00:02:45.101 --> 00:02:49.539
Language contact with Sanskrit and Pali enriched Khmer vocabulary.

42
00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:53.135
Your turn. Say: language contact.

43
00:02:55.835 --> 00:02:57.207
language contact.

44
00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:00.518
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:00.818 --> 00:03:03.526
Rithy, did you know 'nice' once meant 'foolish'?

46
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:08.398
Really? That's a remarkable example of semantic change.

47
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:12.786
And Khmer has its own long history of borrowing.

48
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:19.984
Yes, the many Sanskrit and Pali words in formal Khmer came through Indian religion and court culture.

49
00:03:21.283 --> 00:03:24.339
Plus French: 'kafe', 'ompoul'...

50
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.471
And now the flood of English neologisms used by teenagers on social media!

51
00:03:31.783 --> 00:03:33.853
Some people see that as decline.

52
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.934
But the constant, unstoppable evolution of every living language is completely natural.

53
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.862
Grammar focus: Complex noun phrases (advanced).

54
00:03:47.162 --> 00:03:50.311
Complex noun phrases make descriptions precise.

55
00:03:50.611 --> 00:03:57.710
That's a remarkable example of semantic change. The many Sanskrit and Pali words in formal Khmer came through religion.

56
00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.739
The constant evolution of every living language is natural.

57
00:04:02.039 --> 00:04:10.482
Build long noun phrases in order: determiner, opinion, other adjectives, noun, then prepositional phrase or clause.

58
00:04:11.483 --> 00:04:15.726
Now read and listen. Why Languages Never Stand Still.

59
00:04:16.026 --> 00:04:19.384
Every living language is in a constant state of change.

60
00:04:20.683 --> 00:04:28.256
Over centuries, linguistic drift alters pronunciation and grammar so gradually that speakers rarely notice.

61
00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:35.938
Semantic change transforms meanings: 'nice' once meant 'foolish', and 'silly' once meant 'blessed'.

62
00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:43.348
Grammaticalisation turns ordinary words into grammatical tools, as with the English future 'going to'.

63
00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:50.469
Language contact, through trade, religion and conquest, leads to extensive borrowing.

64
00:04:51.783 --> 00:04:59.704
Khmer, for instance, absorbed thousands of words from Sanskrit, Pali, Thai, French and, increasingly, English.

65
00:05:01.017 --> 00:05:09.808
Meanwhile, neologisms and rapid slang evolution, accelerated by social media, constantly refresh everyday speech.

66
00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:16.088
Language change is therefore not corruption but a sign that a language is alive.

67
00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.989
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:22.591
A newly invented word is a blank.

69
00:05:22.891 --> 00:05:27.624
A, neologism. B, borrowing. C, grammaticalisation.

70
00:05:31.617 --> 00:05:34.731
The answer is A: neologism.

71
00:05:36.217 --> 00:05:38.739
Which is the most complex noun phrase?

72
00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:45.409
A, the fascinating history of Khmer loanwords. B, history. C, the history.

73
00:05:49.417 --> 00:05:53.866
The answer is A: the fascinating history of Khmer loanwords.

74
00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.451
In 'the many French words in modern Khmer', the head noun is blank.

75
00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:03.126
A, words. B, French. C, Khmer.

76
00:06:07.117 --> 00:06:09.035
The answer is A: words.

77
00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.049
A change in what a word means is blank.

78
00:06:13.349 --> 00:06:19.429
A, semantic change. B, linguistic drift. C, language contact.

79
00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:26.194
The answer is A: semantic change.

80
00:06:27.683 --> 00:06:30.972
According to the text, what did 'silly' once mean?

81
00:06:31.272 --> 00:06:35.796
A, blessed. B, foolish. C, small.

82
00:06:39.783 --> 00:06:41.783
The answer is A: blessed.

83
00:06:43.283 --> 00:06:48.548
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is English slang damaging the Khmer language?

84
00:06:53.848 --> 00:06:59.098
Part two: Use at least two complex noun phrases and one term from the unit.

85
00:07:05.398 --> 00:07:18.665
The rapid spread of English slang among Cambodian teenagers is real. However, the long history of borrowing from Sanskrit, Pali and French shows that Khmer adapts. Language change is a sign of life, not decline.

86
00:07:18.965 --> 00:07:21.672
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:22.683 --> 00:07:27.890
Great work today! You learned eight new words and complex noun phrases (advanced).

88
00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:32.711
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:33.011 --> 00:07:36.326
See you in Unit forty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:36.626 --> 00:07:39.928
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

